{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>An olah</b> — for the name of olah. <b>Ishseh</b> — when he slaughters it, he should slaughter it for the name of the fire, and every <b>ishseh</b> means fire. <b>Nicho'ach</b> — restfulness of spirit before Me, for I have spoken and My will is done — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:9). The Rav did not explain what \"for the name of the fire\" means. And in the Gemara (Zevachim 46b) they said: to exclude a flame that is not. And they explained it there to mean that he should slaughter it on condition that he will offer it upon a fire burning in the arrangement of wood, not upon coals that have gone extinguished and are dying out.\n\nBut it appears to me that one must intend that the fire consume it completely — not that it merely roast there a little. This is from their expression in the case of the rebellious and wayward son (Sanhedrin 70a): \"meat that is partially roasted [כִּבְשַׂר כִּיבָא], which thieves eat,\" and in tractate Eruvin (29a): \"and he roasts [וְנִכְבִּיב] and he cooks [וְנֵכוּל].\" And there are those who have a variant reading there (Zevachim 46b): to exclude gebaba that is not — that one should intend to offer it upon a fire of wood, as it is written, \"upon the wood that is upon the fire,\" and not think to offer it upon a fire of straw or of gebaba, in the manner of what we learned (Shabbat 36a): an oven that was heated with straw and with gebaba.\n\nNow, in this verse the reason for the offerings is stated, that they are \"a fire-offering, a pleasing fragrance to HaShem.\" And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:32) that the reason for the offerings is because the Egyptians and the Chaldeans, among whom Israel had been sojourners and settlers in their land from of old, would worship cattle and sheep — for the Egyptians worship the lamb and the Chaldeans worship demons that appear to them in the likeness of goats, and the people of India to this day will never slaughter cattle. Therefore He commanded to slaughter these three species for the name of the Exalted One, so that it would become known that the thing they considered to be the ultimate transgression — that is what they should offer to the Creator, and through it their sins would be atoned. For thus the evil beliefs, which are the diseases of the soul, would be healed, since every disease and every illness is healed only by its opposite. These are his words, and he elaborated on them.\n\nBut these areempty  words [diburim shel havai]. They would heal a great fracture yet pose a great difficulty upon slight reflection: that they make the table of HaShem defiled, as if it were nothing but a means to remove from the hearts of the wicked and foolish of the world — when Scripture says that they are \"food of the fire-offering for a pleasing fragrance\"! Moreover, according to the folly of the Egyptians, their disease would not be healed by this at all but would rather increase the pain, for the thought of the wicked aforementioned is to worship the lamb-constellation and the ox-constellation because they believe these have power within them, according to their thinking — and therefore they do not eat them, out of honor for their power and their principle. But if one slaughters them for the name of the Exalted One, this is to them an honor and an elevation for those very constellations — and they themselves act precisely thus, as it says (Leviticus 17:7), \"and they shall no longer offer their sacrifices to the goats,\" and the makers of the calf slaughtered to it.\n\nAnd the Rav mentions that they would offer to the moon at every new moon, and to the sun when it entered the constellations known to them from their books. And the disease would be healed far more by our eating them to satiety — which is forbidden to them and abhorrent in their eyes, and they would never do such a thing.\n\nNow Noah, when he came out of the ark with his three sons — there was no Chaldean or Egyptian in the world — offered an offering, and it pleased HaShem, and Scripture said of it (Genesis 8:21), \"And HaShem smelled the pleasing fragrance,\" and from it He said to His heart, \"I will never again curse the ground because of man\" (ibid.). \"And Abel, he too brought from the firstborn of his flock and from their fat, and HaShem turned to Abel and to his offering\" (Genesis 4:4) — and there was not yet in the world any trace of idolatry at all. And Balaam said, \"The seven altars have I arranged and offered up a bull and a ram on the altar\" (Numbers 23:4) — and his intention was not now to remove evil beliefs from him, nor was he commanded in this, but he did so to draw near to Elohim so that prophecy would rest upon him.\n\nAnd the language of the offerings is \"My offering, My food, for My fire-offerings, My pleasing fragrance\" (Numbers 28:2) — and it would be unthinkable that there should be in them no benefit and no desire, only the negation of idolatry from the awareness of fools!\n\nIt is more fitting to hear the reason that they state concerning them: because the deeds of human beings are completed in thought, in speech, and in action, HaShem commanded that when one sins, he shall bring an offering, lean his hands upon it corresponding to the deed, and confess with his mouth corresponding to the speech, and burn on the fire the entrails and the kidneys, which are the instruments of thought and desire, and the legs corresponding to the person's hands and feet that do all his work, and sprinkle the blood upon the altar corresponding to his blood in his soul — so that a person, when doing all these things, should reflect that he has sinned against his Elohim with his body and his soul, and that it would have been fitting for his blood to be spilled and his body burned, were it not for the lovingkindness of the Creator, Who took from him a substitute and ransom in this offering, that its blood should be in place of his blood, its life in place of his life, and the chief parts of the offering's limbs corresponding to the chief parts of his limbs, and the portions to sustain the teachers of the Torah who pray on his behalf. And the daily offering is so that the multitudes will never be exempt from sinning constantly. And these matters, which are accepted, draw the heart like the words of Aggadah.\n\nAnd by way of truth, there is in the offerings a hidden secret. You will enter into it from what our Rabbis said in Sifrei (Pinchas 143) and at the end of Menachot (110a): Shimon ben Azzai said: Come and see what is written in the chapter of offerings — that neither \"El\" nor \"your Elohim\" nor \"Elohim\" nor \"Shaddai\" nor \"Tzeva'ot\" is said of them, but only YHWH, the unique Name, so as not to give an opening to the adversary to argue. And should you say: it is for eating that He needs it — Scripture says: \"If I were hungry, I would not tell you, for Mine is the world and its fullness\" (Psalms 50:12). I did not tell you, sacrifice, but rather so that My will should be spoken and done. And at the beginning of Torat Kohanim (Sifra, chapter 2:5), Rabbi Yosi says: Everywhere that \"offering\" is stated, it is stated with YHWH, so as not to give an opening to heretics to descend. These are the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory.\n\nAnd it is true that in the chapter of offerings, neither \"El\" nor \"Elohim\" is stated. But we find: \"and you shall offer upon it burnt offerings to HaShem your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 27:6), and it is written: \"the food of their Elohim they offer\" (Leviticus 21:22), \"and you shall sanctify him, for he offers the food of his Elohim\" (ibid. 21:8), and it is written in the aforementioned psalm: \"Offer to Elohim a thanksgiving sacrifice\" (Psalms 50:14), and it is further written (II Chronicles 29:6–7): \"For our fathers acted treacherously and did what was evil in the eyes of HaShem our Elohim, and they forsaked Him, and they turned their faces away from the dwelling of HaShem, and they turned their backs; and they shut the doors of the porch and extinguished the lamps, and incense they did not burn, and an offering they did not offer up in the sanctuary to the Elohim of Israel.\" But the entire matter is explained in the Torah, as it says (Numbers 28:2), \"My offering, My food, for My fire-offerings,\" and it says (Leviticus 3:11), \"food of fire-offering\" — that they are food for the fire-offering, and from it come the fires, and <b>ishseh</b> means fire.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Leviticus 1:9) that <b>ishseh</b> is a name of an adjective, and its meaning is \"an offering of fire,\" and it is an adjective modifying the word <b>hakol</b> (\"the whole\"), and in the meal-offering where it says (Leviticus 2:2), \"and the priest shall burn its memorial portion, a fire-offering, a pleasing fragrance to HaShem,\" it would be an adjective modifying the handful.\n\nBut this is not so. Rather, <b>ishseh</b> is a noun, like <b>esh</b> (fire), and <b>olah isheh</b> is like \"a burnt offering of fire, a pleasing fragrance to HaShem,\" and so all of them, for their meaning is like \"food of fire-offering\" (below, 3:11, 16). But He did not say <b>esh</b> and said <b>isheh</b> according to its meaning, as He showed you on the mountain at the giving of the Torah. For the offering is in the attribute of judgment, and the slaughter is for the name of HaShem alone, that one should not intend anything else in the world but for the name of HaShem alone.\n\nAnd this is the meaning of \"it is a burnt offering, a fire-offering\" — therefore Scripture says (Leviticus 21:6), \"for the fire-offerings of HaShem, the food of their Elohim, they offer, and they shall be holy,\" for it is to the fire-offerings of HaShem that an offering to Elohim is brought. And therefore they said that in the commandments of the offerings, He did not mention \"El\" or \"Elohim\" but only \"a fire-offering, a pleasing fragrance to HaShem,\" because the intention should be to HaShem alone — the one who brings the offering should not intend, nor let it arise in his thought, anything but the unique Name alone.\n\nAnd this is the statement of the Sages (Sanhedrin 60a): \"Empty out all the forms of worship, all of them, for the sake of the unique Name.\" And in Torat Kohanim (end of chapter 6): \"לַה'\" — for the name of the One Who made the world.\n\nAnd this is what the psalm said: \"Offer to Elohim a thanksgiving sacrifice, and pay your vows to the Most High\" (Psalms 50:14), \"for HaShem is the Most High, awesome, a great king over all the earth\" (ibid. 47:3). And one vows only to His unique Name. And this is what He said: \"I am Elohim your Elohim; I will not reprove you for your sacrifices\" (Psalms 50:7–8), as He said, \"I am HaShem your Elohim\" (Exodus 20:2). And this is the meaning of the entire psalm, as it says, \"El, Elohim, HaShem, He spoke and summoned the earth\" (Psalms 50:1) — He mentioned the full Name over the full world, and mentioned in it the offerings.\n\nAnd this is what is said (Isaiah 60:7): \"They shall go up with acceptance upon My altar, and the house of My glory I will glorify\" — to say that the offerings shall be upon the acceptance, which is His altar, and the house of His glory He will glorify when they go up for a pleasing fragrance.\n\nNow, <b>nicho'ach</b> is from \"the spirit of Elijah settled upon Elisha\" (II Kings 2:15), \"and the spirit rested upon them\" (Numbers 11:26). And every offering [קָרְבָּן] is an expression of drawing near [קְרִיבָה] and unification [אַחְדוּת]. And therefore he said: \"and an offering they did not offer up in the sanctuary to the Elohim of Israel,\" for the offering in the sanctuary must be to the Elohim of Israel.\n\nAnd the angel taught Manoah the matter of offerings. He said: \"If you detain me, I will not eat of your bread\" (Judges 13:16) — that if he makes bread for him, he will not accept it from him, because it is an unfit thing [piggul] and an abominable sacrifice to the Name. But if you make an offering to HaShem alone, you may offer it up, and it will be acceptable upon the fire of HaShem. And then it went up in the flame of the altar. And so the matter is explicit and explained.\n\nAnd may the Good One grant atonement:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The opening reads \"והינן דברי הבאי\" — literally \"these are words of emptiness/futility\"; translated as \"these are empty words [diburim shel havai].\" I rendered the phrase as Ramban's dismissal of Maimonides' rationale while preserving the Hebrew connotation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:24:51.007762788Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}